---
import Comparison from "../../../components/studies/derelict-three/Comparison.svelte";
import Damage from "../../../components/studies/derelict-three/Damage.svelte";
import Progressive from "../../../components/studies/derelict-three/Progressive.svelte";
import ShardMotion from "../../../components/studies/derelict-three/ShardMotion.svelte";
import { studies } from "../../../data/studies";
import StudyLayout from "../../../layouts/StudyLayout.astro";
import "@void-drift/core/styles/buttons.css";

const study = studies.find(
  (entry) => entry.slug === "derelict-three-2026-11-06",
);
---

<StudyLayout study={study}>
  <aside aria-label="Study outcome">
    <h2>Completed / Visual direction accepted</h2>
    <p>
      Closed on 6 November 2026. The selected direction is the supplied artwork
      at the working 110-unit extent, eight-piece B distributed shedding,
      ejected energy and the expanding release pulse. Parts 03 and 04 define
      readiness and implementation scope. The damage comparisons remain
      unselected; closure does not turn their thresholds into gameplay
      requirements.
    </p>
  </aside>
  <section>
    <h2>01 / A broken monument, still alive inside</h2>
    <p>
      A passing ship encounters a remnant of something vastly larger: two long,
      fractured hulls joined underneath by darker material. The supplied
      silhouette and its warm-to-cool, pale mineral colours give the structure
      its identity. Inside, an enlarged hollow square turns independently,
      throwing broad light across the break while its centre remains black. The
      structure is broken; its energy is not spent.
    </p>
    <p>
      The design tension is between the weight of the remnant and the invitation
      of its energy. During flight, the hull must remain a recognisable place
      with an interior, rather than disappear behind a bright marker.
      Destruction should change that relationship: solid material becomes
      physical shards, and contained energy becomes something the player can
      pursue. The appearance, timing and separation of those events remain open.
    </p>
    <p>
      This cycle follows the draft into an encounter, then into rupture and
      pursuit. Relative size supports the investigation; 110 world units is the
      provisional working extent established in 02.2. Wasp and Fiddler provide
      contrasting familiar bodies against which to judge presence. Production
      gameplay remains unchanged.
    </p>
  </section>
  <details>
    <summary>
      Retained reference / hulls, light and initial Wasp comparison
    </summary>
    <img
      src="/studies/derelict-draft-two-2026-11-06/retained-artwork.png"
      alt="Retained draft artwork with two fractured hulls, a dark connecting structure and hollow luminous square"
    >
    <p>
      The exact Inkscape paths, material colours, slightly enlarged rotating
      square, broad glow and black hollow centre carry forward.
      <a href="/lab/studies/derelict-draft-two-2026-11-06#ship-scale"
        >The initial Wasp comparison</a
      >
      remains useful evidence of proportion; none of its sizes was accepted.
      Both earlier studies remain intact.
    </p>
  </details>
  <details>
    <summary>02.1 / A structure around the energy</summary>
    <h2>02.1 / A structure around the energy</h2>
    <p>
      The controlled comparison with Wasp and Fiddler ruled out extents above
      120 world units as too large. It did not settle whether 120 itself is too
      large. The hull geometry, material colours and hollow energy square remain
      the reference.
    </p>
    <details>
      <summary>Retained comparison / 120 and 300 units</summary>
      <img
        src="/studies/derelict-three-2026-11-06/controlled-120-300.png"
        alt="Previous controlled comparison: identical Wasp and Fiddler beside 120- and 300-unit derelicts"
      >
    </details>
  </details>
  <details id="proportion">
    <summary>02.2 / Working extent: 110 units</summary>
    <h2>02.2 / Between 100 and 120</h2>
    <p>
      The 80, 100 and 120 comparison narrowed the useful range to between 100
      and 120 world units. The midpoint, 110, now sits between those same
      boundary references. Wasp and Fiddler stay identical in every group. Only
      the derelict changes size; its geometry, colours, heading and energy phase
      stay fixed. The question is which extent gives the broken structure
      sufficient presence without overwhelming the ships.
    </p>
    <Comparison client:load />
    <details>
      <summary>Retained comparison / 80, 100 and 120</summary>
      <img
        src="/studies/derelict-three-2026-11-06/controlled-80-100-120.png"
        alt="Controlled 80, 100 and 120 comparison that narrowed the desired derelict extent to between 100 and 120 world units"
      >
    </details>
    <p>
      The outer-glow switch and energy-phase control affect all three together.
      110 world units is the working extent for the next exploration, following
      visual review. It remains provisional. This comparison concerns appearance
      and proportion; physical shards, rupture and released energy remain open.
    </p>
  </details>
  <details>
    <summary>02.3 / Release direction — ejection retained</summary>
    <p>
      Ejecting the energy is preferred to leaving it at the rupture point. The
      four-part split established that comparison, but its fracture shapes were
      not convincing and are not the fracture direction.
    </p>
    <img
      src="/studies/derelict-three-2026-11-06/energy-release.png"
      alt="Retained comparison of stationary and ejecting energy; ejection was preferred"
    >
  </details>
  <details id="splinter-count">
    <summary>02.4 / Eight-piece progressive splintering</summary>
    <h2>02.4 / Shedding before containment fails</h2>
    <p>
      The same 110-unit structure breaks into 2, 5 or 8 unequal pieces. Cuts
      follow uneven sites along the narrow hulls: long remnants, shorter hull
      sections and small tips. All original material remains accounted for.
      Ejection is retained in every proposal.
    </p>
    <Progressive client:load />
    <p>
      At 0.70 seconds, the five- and eight-piece proposals have shed tips and
      edge sections while the energy stays inside the remaining structure. The
      two-piece proposal holds together until release. At 1.00 seconds, the
      remaining pieces separate and the energy ejects along the same path in all
      three. Small early pieces travel farther; larger remnants move more
      slowly.
    </p>
    <p>
      This tests whether gradual loss of material gives the final release a
      readable cause, and whether additional fragments enrich the breakup or
      obscure it. The asymmetric fracture shapes and release order are
      proposals, not accepted artwork. Bodies use production salvage movement
      and lifetime; the existing circular collision proxies still need to be
      fitted to the shard shapes before collision or collection risk can be
      judged.
    </p>
  </details>
  <details id="shard-motion">
    <summary>02.5 / Distributed shedding selected</summary>
    <h2>02.5 / Eight pieces, individual motion</h2>
    <p>
      Eight-piece progressive splintering is the preferred direction. A retains
      that reference. B changes only the early shedding locations: a forward
      tip, a side section and a lower end. C keeps B’s release order and gives
      each piece its own drift and spin, including clockwise, counterclockwise
      and nearly steady remnants. Energy ejection stays identical throughout.
    </p>
    <ShardMotion client:load />
    <p>
      Compare A with B during early shedding, then B with C after release. The
      proposed variation is fixed and repeatable. Hull geometry, total material,
      110-unit extent and viewing scale remain unchanged. B is selected:
      distributed shedding with its existing drift and spin. C’s added motion
      variation is not selected. Production gameplay has not changed.
    </p>
  </details>
  <section id="release-cue">
    <h2>02.6 / The instant containment fails</h2>
    <p>
      B’s distributed shedding is retained: eight pieces, early loss from tip,
      side and lower edge, followed by energy ejection. These three views share
      that exact breakup. Only the release cue changes.
    </p>
    <ShardMotion
      client:load
      releaseCues
      label="Identical B breakup with movement alone, a brief flash, or an expanding pulse"
    />
    <p>
      Movement alone leaves the separating hull and departing square to carry
      the event. The flash adds a compact light burst for 0.10 seconds. The
      pulse expands from the rupture point for 0.28 seconds. Both fade with the
      shared light persistence; neither changes the energy, shards or their
      motion.
    </p>
    <p>
      Release shows the first 0.08 seconds after containment fails. Afterglow
      exposes how long each cue competes with the energy being released. Play
      from intact to judge the event in sequence, or scrub and pause all three
      together. The expanding pulse is selected. Movement alone and the brief
      flash remain comparison evidence.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="encounter">
    <h2>02.7 / What does each hit change?</h2>
    <p>
      Each remnant receives the same five equal hits, worth 20 damage apiece. A
      holds together until 100 damage. B sheds a tip, side section and lower end
      at 20, 40 and 60, then releases the energy at 100. C sheds the same pieces
      but loses containment at 80; the remaining hull breaks at 100.
    </p>
    <Damage client:load />
    <p>
      At three hits, compare whether visible shedding makes accumulated damage
      understandable. At four hits, C offers the energy while five hull pieces
      remain attached: pursuing the prize can replace finishing the structure.
      At five hits, all three have released identical material. The selected
      pulse accompanies actual energy release in each proposal.
    </p>
    <p>
      The 110-unit artwork, eight fracture shapes, B’s shard velocities and
      spin, ejection impulse and expanding pulse are fixed. Only damage
      thresholds differ. Hits are a repeatable study fixture, not a weapon
      balance proposal; these values are not production health or damage
      settings. Hit location is intentionally held out: this experiment compares
      total-damage rules, not local armour damage.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="readiness">
    <h2>03 / Feasible visuals; damage and collision decisions remain</h2>
    <p>
      The selected reference is 02.6’s expanding-pulse view: the supplied hull
      geometry and colours, enlarged hollow square and broad glow, provisional
      110-unit extent, eight-piece B breakup, distributed early shedding and
      ejected energy. The pulse expands at the rupture point for 0.28 seconds
      and then decays with the existing light surface. The retained example uses
      seed 2026, fixed heading, viewing scale 1.60 and a repeatable two-second
      sequence. Early releases occur at 0.25, 0.45 and 0.65 seconds; containment
      fails at 1.00. Those times demonstrate the visual sequence, not damage
      rules.
    </p>
    <p>
      The artwork, fracture partitions and release cue are demonstrated. Current
      salvage already supplies moving bodies, lifetimes, weapon hits and energy
      collection; the effects surface supplies light persistence. Production
      destruction currently emits four generic fragments and a cell immediately.
      It does not yet represent a partially broken hull or staged containment
      loss.
    </p>
    <p>
      The remaining implementation decisions are: what damage releases each
      piece and the energy; how collision boundaries follow the remaining hull
      and long shards; and integration of the released energy with the owning
      mode’s existing reward, lifetime and collection rules. Eight pieces is the
      selected visual reference, not approval for randomly choosing two through
      eight. 02.7 compares three damage policies, but no policy was selected.
      Damage-triggered timing and variation need an explicit implementation
      decision. Performance with several simultaneous breakups remains
      unmeasured. Visual implementation is feasible with the existing renderer
      and effects systems. Full gameplay integration is not ready until damage
      progression and shape-matched collision are specified.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="scope">
    <h2>04 / Deliver the selected reference in bounded slices</h2>
    <p>
      <strong
        >Art-direction decision required before production promotion:</strong
      >
      the retained Inkscape colour treatment includes a hue overlay. Production
      hulls currently require flat fills in docs/design.md. Resolve whether to
      bake the selected colours into flat planes or explicitly approve a scoped
      exception. This study does not change that principle.
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li>
        <strong>Shared visual definition — small.</strong>
        Promote the selected 110-unit artwork, eight fracture regions and pulse
        into shared renderers. Keep the study as the visual reference; verify
        intact and separated silhouettes, material continuity and fixed-scale
        ship comparisons.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Damage and physical bodies — medium.</strong>
        After agreeing the damage contract, define validated state for attached
        and released pieces, containment and energy release. Adapt salvage
        simulation and collision geometry. Verify material is released once,
        retained hulls remain solid, and collisions agree with visible shapes.
        This depends on slice one.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Energy and release feedback — small to medium.</strong>
        Connect the pulse to actual containment loss and the selected square to
        the moving collectible. Preserve B’s ejection reference. Verify
        collection, expiry, pause and replay; reuse the owning mode’s reward,
        lifetime and collection contract unless separately changed. This depends
        on slices one and two.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Integrated trial — medium.</strong>
        Exercise the agreed encounter in test flight and its Lab reference.
        Update the owning Derelicts mode contract, test-flight reference and
        effects contract alongside implementation. The current Derelicts spec
        already records B’s visual direction and eight physical pieces;
        reconcile the selected pulse and damage decision without changing
        unrelated mode rules. Check keyboard and landscape touch, multiple
        breakups, body/effect budgets and measured frame time. Human review
        judges feel and visual fidelity separately from correctness.
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      The study is completed with the visual direction accepted and the
      implementation scope defined. No A/B/C damage policy in 02.7 is selected.
      Production implementation remains separate work; no production code, push
      or deployment is authorized by this closure.
    </p>
  </section>
</StudyLayout>
<style>
details {
  margin-block: var(--spacing-xl);
}
img {
  display: block;
  max-width: 100%;
  max-height: 680px;
  object-fit: contain;
  margin-block: var(--spacing-lg);
}
</style>
